<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
	Cleartext HTTP, permitted for loopback and nothing else (§4.5, §13 Q26).

	Android blocks cleartext outright from API 28. The app needs exactly one exception: its own
	loopback map-pack server, which hands MapLibre the bytes of a downloaded PMTiles archive by
	range request (LoopbackMapPackServer). A file on disk is not something the WebView can address,
	so an HTTP server on 127.0.0.1 is how the renderer reaches it.

	DELIBERATELY NOT `android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"`. That switch turns cleartext back on for
	every host the app will ever talk to, including the API and the tile servers, and it would do
	so silently and permanently. This grants it per domain, which is the narrowest form the
	platform offers.

	Nothing here is reachable from off the device: 127.0.0.1 is this app's own process, and
	10.0.2.2 is the Android emulator's private shortcut to the developer's machine - it resolves to
	nothing on real hardware, which is why it is safe to leave in a release build rather than
	maintaining two configs. It is listed because MauiConstants documents it as the way to point a
	debug build at a server running on the developer's laptop.
-->
<network-security-config>
	<domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
		<domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
		<domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
		<domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
	</domain-config>

	<!--
		The map-pack catalogue host (§4.2), and the one entry here that is genuinely off-device.

		⚠ TEMPORARY, AND IT SHOULD BE DELETED RATHER THAN INHERITED. The host answers on 443 with a
		certificate issued for another domain, so HTTPS to it cannot be verified; this exception is
		what lets the Maps screen read the list at all in the meantime. The moment that host serves
		a certificate matching its own name, this block goes and MapPackCatalogue.DefaultUrl becomes
		https - there is nothing else to change.

		What it exposes is both the list and the archives - the packs are served from this same host,
		so a pack download is cleartext too (MapPackCatalogue.PermitsCleartext). That is the part
		worth being uncomfortable about: hundreds of megabytes the app renders as the map a rider
		navigates by, open to being rewritten in flight, and the catalogue's published sha256 is not
		yet compared against what arrives (offline-maps-plan.md §4.3 step 3). Doing that check is
		what makes this exception survivable rather than merely convenient.

		Scoped to the single host and NOT includeSubdomains, so nothing else the app talks to - the
		API above all - loses its transport security to this. The same host is named in
		MapPackCatalogue.CleartextHost and in Info.plist; the three have to agree.
	-->
	<domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
		<domain includeSubdomains="false">pmtiles.securehub.net</domain>
	</domain-config>
</network-security-config>
